  "body": "## Finding description and impact.\nRoot Cause.\nThe `rebalanceFunds` function in `FastAccessVault.sol` is publicly callable without any access control. This allows anyone to trigger the rebalancing, which can interfere with the vault's balance during redemption operations.\n```solidity\nfunction rebalanceFunds() external {  //    No access control - anyone can call\n    uint256 aumReferenceValue = _issuerContract.getCollateralUnderCustody();\n    uint256 targetBalance = _calculateTargetBufferBalance(aumReferenceValue);\n    uint256 currentBalance = _vaultToken.balanceOf(address(this));\n\n    if (currentBalance < targetBalance) {\n        uint256 needed = targetBalance - currentBalance;\n        emit TopUpRequestedFromCustodian(address(custodian), needed, targetBalance);\n    } else if (currentBalance > targetBalance) {\n        uint256 excess = currentBalance - targetBalance;\n        if (!_vaultToken.transfer(custodian, excess)) {  //    Transfers funds away\n            revert CommonErrors.TransferFailed();\n        }\n        emit ExcessFundsTransferredToCustodian(address(custodian), excess, targetBalance);\n    }\n}\n```\nDescription:\nThe `rebalanceFunds` function is intended to adjust the vault's balance to a target level based on the total collateral under custody. However, because it is external and has no access control, an attacker can call it at any time. In particular, an attacker can front-run a user's redemption transaction. If the vault's current balance is above the target, the rebalancing will transfer the excess to the custodian, potentially reducing the vault's balance below the amount required for the pending redemption. This causes the redemption to fail, resulting in a loss of gas fees and a denial of service for the user.\n\n# Attack Vectors:\n1. An attacker monitors the mempool for redemption transactions from the `iTryIssuer` contract.\n2. When a redemption transaction is seen, the attacker checks the vault's current balance and the target balance. If the vault has excess funds, the attacker front-runs the redemption by calling `rebalanceFunds` to transfer the excess to the custodian.\n3. The redemption transaction then fails due to insufficient balance in the vault.\n\n# Impact:\n\n1. Denial of service for legitimate redemptions.\n2. Loss of user funds due to failed transactions (gas fees).\n3. Potential for repeated attacks leading to a protocol-wide liquidity crisis or \"bank run\" scenario.\n\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\n * Access Control.\n```solidity\n// Add modifier to restrict access\nfunction rebalanceFunds() external onlyIssuerOrOwner {\n    // ... existing logic ...\n}\n\n// Add new modifier\nmodifier onlyIssuerOrOwner() {\n    if (msg.sender != address(_issuerContract) && msg.sender != owner()) {\n        revert UnauthorizedCaller(msg.sender);\n    }\n    _;\n}\n```\n* Enhanced Fix - With Safety Checks.\n```solidity\nfunction rebalanceFunds() external onlyIssuerOrOwner {\n    uint256 aumReferenceValue = _issuerContract.getCollateralUnderCustody();\n    uint256 targetBalance = _calculateTargetBufferBalance(aumReferenceValue);\n    uint256 currentBalance = _vaultToken.balanceOf(address(this));\n\n    // Safety check: Don't rebalance if it would drop below safe threshold\n    uint256 safeMinimum = minimumExpectedBalance * 120 / 100; // 20% buffer\n    \n    if (currentBalance > targetBalance) {\n        uint256 excess = currentBalance - targetBalance;\n        \n        // Ensure we don't go below safe minimum after transfer\n        if (currentBalance - excess >= safeMinimum) {\n            if (!_vaultToken.transfer(custodian, excess)) {\n                revert CommonErrors.TransferFailed();\n            }\n            emit ExcessFundsTransferredToCustodian(address(custodian), excess, targetBalance);\n        }\n    } else if (currentBalance < targetBalance) {\n        uint256 needed = targetBalance - currentBalance;\n        emit TopUpRequestedFromCustodian(address(custodian), needed, targetBalance);\n    }\n}\n```\n* Add Rebalancing Cooldown.\n* Add Minimum Transfer Threshold.",
